-- Real photographs for the generated catalogue, replacing the coloured text
-- placeholders on about a quarter of the 50,000 rows.
--
-- Sources are the two that are genuinely free to use: DummyJSON
-- (https://dummyjson.com), published for demo shops, and StockSnap via
-- Openverse (https://openverse.org) under CC0. tools/catalogue_images.json
-- holds the same mapping for freshly generated catalogues, with credits.
--
-- Every photo was reviewed by eye against the line it illustrates. Lines with
-- no honest photograph - Indian ethnic wear, household cleaning supplies, DVD
-- box sets - are deliberately absent and keep their labelled tile, which is
-- better than showing an evening gown for a printed kurti.
--
-- A photo shows the KIND of product, not the exact model: a Redmi Note 13 gets
-- a real smartphone photograph, not that phone.
--
-- The generator encodes the product line into the placeholder URL as
-- ?text=<Product+Line>, so that is what rows are matched on. Where a line has
-- several photographs, product_id modulo the count spreads them across the
-- line instead of giving two thousand products the same picture.
--
-- Only placeholders are replaced, so a photo an administrator has already set
-- is left alone.

UPDATE product p
   SET image = photo.url
  FROM (VALUES
    ('Car+Battery'          , 0, 1, 'https://cdn.stocksnap.io/img-thumbs/960w/T7T37KWY8G.jpg'),
